WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:11.200
We move on now, Paul, to the NHS England Finance

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.920
Report, month 10.
Paul Baumann&gt;&gt; Thank you very much. There

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:19.730
are three reasons to keep this short today,
one of which is that the Chief Executive,

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:23.320
with the assistance of the Deputy Chair, have
ruined all my best lines already in the meeting,

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:28.859
to an extent which is even more comprehensive
than on normal occasions. The second is that

00:00:28.859 --> 00:00:32.500
I am very keen to save plenty of time for
the Greater Manchester item that we are about

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.620
to come to; partly because, frankly, it has
been a good opportunity for a poor accountant

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:41.820
to get out of the office a bit more, so it
is an interesting topic to spend time on.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:46.180
The third is, if you will take this in the
spirit in which it is intended, I as always

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:50.200
value your collective wisdom on how we can
improve our financial delivery, but given

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:55.080
that I have six hours of working time left
this year to implement your wisdom, the return

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:19.770
on investment may be relatively marginal.
In a feeble attempt to return to the numbers

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:24.759
at this point, last month I presented the
month nine numbers, which showed, as you will

00:01:24.759 --> 00:01:30.549
recall, I am sure, a projected underspend
after risks and mitigations of £413 million.

00:01:30.549 --> 00:01:33.920
I said at the time that we would continue
to seek every opportunity we could find to

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.780
improve on that position in order to support
the overall position within the health sector,

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:44.350
and I am pleased to say that our efforts in
the last month have borne some fruit. You

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:47.929
will see that the core forecast has risen
in the papers you have in front of you to

00:01:47.929 --> 00:01:53.840
£440 million of underspend in year, with
the position after risks and mitigations increasing

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:59.939
to £478 million. These are in the scheme
of things relatively small, but nonetheless

00:01:59.939 --> 00:02:06.509
very welcome movements. Perhaps equally important,
though, is that the financial performance

00:02:06.509 --> 00:02:11.039
of CCGs and indeed of direct commissioning
teams, including specialised commissioning,

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:16.530
has been very stable in recent months following
the in-depth review that we did at month nine

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:20.939
and reported on at the last Board meeting.
The improvement in month ten has in fact come

00:02:20.939 --> 00:02:25.480
about predominantly from higher underspends
in central programme costs, including, I would

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:31.010
say, the benefits from rate rebates, which
now total £60 million in year.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:35.010
I can also report, with the benefit now of
an extra week, which I was not able to reflect

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:41.390
in the Board report, that we have looked at
month 11 reporting, which shows continuity

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:45.470
of that stability of performance. The figures
came through last week – just too late,

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:49.560
as I say, to catch the Board papers – but
the net position after risks and mitigations

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:56.299
has risen slightly from the £478 million
you have in front of you to £494 million;

00:02:56.299 --> 00:03:01.720
if you like, a rounded version would be £500
million, or £0.5 billion of underspend. Again,

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.450
the core financial performance of the commissioning
system is pretty well unchanged from the numbers

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:11.519
that you have in front of you. As the finance
teams around the country sharpen their quill

00:03:11.519 --> 00:03:17.610
pens and stock up on their – sugar-free,
Simon – energy drinks ahead of the year-end

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:22.610
accounting marathon which lies ahead of them,
I think we can be pretty confident that we

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:29.860
will push the underspend in the commissioning
system to round about that £500 million mark,

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:36.379
which is an improvement on what we were committed
to when we last spoke. This is obviously particularly

00:03:36.379 --> 00:03:42.390
critical because it further strengthens our
contribution to the strenuous efforts which

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:47.250
are going on across the whole health sector
to offset the significant increase in the

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:53.950
provider deficit position in the Health Service
in 2015/2016.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:57.730
That sounds altogether too positive to be
left there, for an accountant at least, so

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:01.879
I do need just to give you the usual health
warning, if that is not too much of a pun,

00:04:01.879 --> 00:04:07.420
on those numbers, which is that, true as they
are, they reflect quite a number of non-recurrent

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:12.939
factors which we cannot build on in our interpretation
of where we are headed to in 2016/2017. So

00:04:12.939 --> 00:04:17.630
if you take the underspend as being the £500
million I have just talked about, all of that

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:23.600
is accounted for by three factors, which are
inherently non-recurrent. Roughly £200 million

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:27.350
of it is the legacy continuing healthcare
claims which, as we have talked about a number

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:31.900
of times during the course of the year, have
been significantly lower than the level of

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:37.669
spend that we had forecast for the year. That
will catch up over time. Secondly, we have

00:04:37.669 --> 00:04:42.580
brought down our allocation of money for that
purpose, for next year, to the level that

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:47.240
we think it will run at, so we are not expecting
to have significant headroom in that area

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:51.199
in 2016/2017.
In a similar way, the central programme costs

00:04:51.199 --> 00:04:56.490
are round about another £200 million of the
£500 million, and if you go back to the business

00:04:56.490 --> 00:05:01.240
plan presentation that we started the day
with, we have brought our central programme

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:07.830
costs down by 10% in an underlying sense this
year, and a number of the non-recurrent sources

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:12.000
of central programme costs that we had in
2015/2016 are not available to us because

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:17.610
we prioritised all of the money that we could
for both the frontline challenge that we have

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:23.199
in 2016/2017, and the task of transformation,
which we can discuss in a different context.

00:05:23.199 --> 00:05:27.199
Thirdly, rates rebates: who knows quite where
that will go next year, but it is not something

00:05:27.199 --> 00:05:32.340
that you can build into recurrent budgets.
For all of those reasons, it is good that

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:37.199
we have the £500 million this year, it is
helpful in the context of the overall balance,

00:05:37.199 --> 00:05:44.380
but it is not something that should give us
any particular cause for confidence, really,

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:49.020
around having all of that available to tackle
the big challenges we have for next year,

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:54.190
which is really where I wanted just to finish
this particular session. We are, as I think

00:05:54.190 --> 00:06:01.629
I commented last month, in the middle of a
process of getting to a robust set of financial

00:06:01.629 --> 00:06:06.970
and operational plans across both the commissioning
sector and the providing sector. Absolutely

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:11.259
critical to that is the partnership, which
is a significantly strengthened partnership

00:06:11.259 --> 00:06:17.240
that we have, with NHS Improvement as it comes
together in all of this. Clearly, there is

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:22.270
a well-rehearsed challenge in the provider
sector of managing to get down to a balanced

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:27.949
provider sector with the benefit of the £1.8
billion of sustainability funding which has

00:06:27.949 --> 00:06:34.699
been put into the settlement for 2016/2017,
but given that the exit recurrent deficit

00:06:34.699 --> 00:06:39.940
or underlying deficit in the provider sector
is probably closer to £2.8 billion than it

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:45.009
is to £1.8 billion, I think there is a very
big challenge for the provider sector in getting

00:06:45.009 --> 00:06:51.300
its efficiencies sufficient to bring that
particular set of numbers into balance, and

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:57.220
you will be aware of the work that is being
done in particular by NHSI colleagues to sort

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:01.590
all of that through with the Sustainability
and Transformation Fund and its various elements

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:05.569
of conditionality.
There is no less a challenge, however, for

00:07:05.569 --> 00:07:10.879
commissioners in all of that, partly because
commissioners have a big role to play in helping

00:07:10.879 --> 00:07:15.099
health economies as a whole to deal with the
challenge I have just been talking about,

00:07:15.099 --> 00:07:20.479
partly – with thanks to my friends for spoiling
it – because we have this issue of a potential

00:07:20.479 --> 00:07:26.789
backlog of RTT activity that we have to make
sure is in the plans of CCGs in terms of funding

00:07:26.789 --> 00:07:33.569
for that activity, and in the plans of providers
in terms of the capacity then to deliver it.

00:07:33.569 --> 00:07:40.740
Thirdly, we have to make sure that risk management
is in place around this, to an extent that

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:47.240
we have not had in previous years, partly
because there have been other places to go

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:51.580
when the money has got short. That has been
sorely tested this year and, as I have mentioned

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:56.080
already, it is a tight balance, but given
that all of the money that was available for

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:01.580
the settlement for health has been put into
the NHS mandate, we have to make sure that

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:07.900
within the NHS mandate we have risk management
firmly in place. A concrete example of that

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:12.970
in the plans that CCGs and our direct commissioning
teams on primary care are putting together

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:18.080
is that they have been asked to set aside
1% – which they always do, for non-recurrent,

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:22.639
largely transformational purposes – but
they have been asked to hold that uncommitted

00:08:22.639 --> 00:08:28.599
at the beginning of the year, because it will
also need to function as a potential risk

00:08:28.599 --> 00:08:33.800
management buffer, with each health economy
needing to demonstrate that it has its finances

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:42.519
planned and then delivering to balance – or
to control total, at least – before it is

00:08:42.519 --> 00:08:47.320
released for that purpose. That is a significant
challenge for many health economies where

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:54.010
that 1% needs to be created, if you like,
before it can be set aside in that way.

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:58.290
For all of those reasons, we are spending
a lot of time at the moment pressure-testing

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:03.339
the sets of plans. Tomorrow, I will be going
through, in meticulous detail, all the CCG

00:09:03.339 --> 00:09:08.260
plans for the whole country to see to what
extent we are getting close to that. I would

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:12.910
not say that the process is by any means complete
yet; we are expecting the formal returns on

00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:18.440
all of this, both on the provider side and
the commissioner side, on 11th April. Clearly,

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:22.570
I will be able to update the Board at the
next meeting as to where that has got to,

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:28.180
but as I said, there is lots of work to do
to make sure we have plausible, affordable

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:35.149
and well-aligned plans, both between providers
and commissioners and between finance activity,

00:09:35.149 --> 00:09:38.140
performance metrics and, critically, quality.
That is a big challenge for the whole system.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:44.110
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Thank you, Paul. Let me invite
comments or queries.

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:49.000
David Roberts&gt;&gt; Just really a comment from
the perspective of the Audit Committee, Malcolm.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:55.120
Firstly, I think it is to the credit of Paul
and his team that the forecast has remained

00:09:55.120 --> 00:10:01.970
pretty stable. That is always a good indication
that things are under control. The second

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:09.120
thing is that it is clear there is considerable
stress and strain across the health economy

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:15.850
generally when it comes to money. Therefore,
the Audit Committee, through both its internal

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:22.690
audit support and the National Audit Office,
are alive to pressures that may be emerging

00:10:22.690 --> 00:10:28.320
or placed onto anyone in the system. There
is no evidence of that at all from our perspective,

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:33.610
but we are alive to it and we will make sure
that we have appropriate checks and balances

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:40.670
in place. Thirdly, just to reiterate what
Paul has said, which is that unfortunately

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:47.560
past performance is no predictor of future
success. Actually, all of the £0.5 billion

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:52.910
of surplus is essentially one-off in nature.
Given the challenges that we now face, the

00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:59.480
Board should be very clear that the stresses
on the commissioning sector, never mind the

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:05.029
provider sector, in 2016/17 will be the highest
they have ever been. We need to be ready for

00:11:05.029 --> 00:11:08.250
that, and that is why the risk management
point that Paul made is so important.

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:14.470
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Thank you, David. Other questions
or comments to Paul? No? Well, thank you.

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:21.149
I always find these finance reports very uplifting,
in a sort of Wagnerian way.

00:11:21.149 --> 00:11:26.410
David Roberts&gt;&gt; You will observe on Twitter
that Paul has just been called a ‘finance deity’.

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:29.420
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Götterdämmerung was more my line!

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:30.540
David Roberts&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:34.820
Paul Baumann&gt;&gt; I wonder if that is a misprint
within the report?

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:39.029
Malcolm Grant&gt;&gt; Right. Well, thank you, Paul.
May we note the financial position for month

00:11:39.029 --> 00:11:43.789
ten as updated orally to month 11? Good. Thank
you very much.

